<% content_for :title, "Lexxy: collaborative rich text for Rails" %>
<% content_for :description, "Make Basecamp's Lexxy editor collaborative with lexxy-realtime: a model macro, a form helper, and a generator. Real-time rich text editing in Rails, with the document rendered back into your record's column as HTML. Built on yrby." %>
<% content_for :canonical, canonical_url("/lexxy") %>

<section class="pt-6 pb-14 sm:pt-14">
  <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-5xl">
    Lexxy, collaborative.
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-zinc-400">
    Lexxy is Basecamp's rich text editor for Rails, the successor to the
    Action Text editor. <code>lexxy-realtime</code> makes it collaborative
    with a model macro, a form helper, and a generator. Two people can
    type in the same field and see each other's keystrokes and carets.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-4">
    <div>
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-500">app/models/post.rb</p>
      <%= sample_lexxy_model %>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-500">app/views/posts/_form.html.erb</p>
      <%= sample_lexxy_form %>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="mb-1 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-500">terminal</p>
      <%= sample_lexxy_install %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Implement <code>authorized?</code> in the channel the generator wrote.
    Until you do, every subscriber is denied. Then open the form in two
    browsers and type.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">
    <code>post.body</code> stays a normal attribute.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    The live document is CRDT state in your database, stored as yrby's
    <code>Y::Document</code> rows. After each recorded change,
    lexxy-realtime renders it to HTML with <code>Y::Lexxy</code> and
    writes the result into the attribute. The HTML matches what the editor
    itself would save, byte for byte. Search indexes it, mailers render
    it, and read-only views show it, the same as any other column. No
    browser is involved in rendering it.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4">
    <%= link_to "Watch the stored HTML update live →", demo_path("lexxy"), rel: "nofollow",
                class: "text-accent underline decoration-rose-400/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-rose-400" %>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">
    Access is a signed grant, per field.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    The form helper signs a GlobalID scoped to that record and attribute. The
    channel looks the record up from it and calls your
    <code>authorized?</code>. A token for <code>:body</code> can't open
    any other field, and a tampered token doesn't resolve to anything. The
    page decides which document the client gets.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">
    Your custom nodes survive.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Custom Lexical nodes you've registered sync like everything else. The
    server renderer takes rules for them
    (<code>has_collaborative_rich_text :body, nodes: { ... }</code>). A
    node without a rule still renders its text into the stored HTML, and
    the renderer logs a warning naming the node types it didn't recognize,
    so CI can catch one you forgot to map.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Encrypted fields work the way Action Text's do:
    <code>has_collaborative_rich_text :body, encrypted: true</code> stores the
    CRDT state through Active Record encryption.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-zinc-800/70 py-14">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-zinc-100">
    Under the hood, it's yrby.
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    lexxy-realtime is a thin layer over the stack this site documents: the
    y-websocket protocol over Action Cable or AnyCable, changes recorded
    before they're acknowledged, and server-side rendering in Ruby.
    Importmap apps get everything from the gem. Apps with a bundler add
    the <code>lexxy-realtime</code> npm package. In both cases the
    collaboration code uses the Lexical build that Lexxy already ships, so
    the page has one editor runtime.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-5 space-y-1 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-500">
    <p><span class="text-accent">bundle add lexxy-realtime</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class="text-accent">bin/rails generate lexxy_realtime:install</span></p>
  </div>
  <nav class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm text-zinc-400">
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://github.com/jpcamara/lexxy-realtime">GitHub</a>
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://rubygems.org/gems/lexxy-realtime">RubyGems</a>
    <a class="hover:text-accent" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/lexxy-realtime">npm</a>
    <%= link_to "Live demo", demo_path("lexxy"), rel: "nofollow", class: "hover:text-accent" %>
    <%= link_to "yrby docs", doc_path("getting-started"), class: "hover:text-accent" %>
  </nav>
</section>
